```

**Key Insight**: Start with manual testing tools like Postman to understand your API, then automate with programming language-specific libraries as your test suite grows.

Every API test follows a similar structure: Arrange, Act, Assert (AAA pattern). Understanding this pattern helps you write clean, maintainable tests.

**The AAA Pattern**:

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 1. ARRANGE - Set up test conditions │
│    - Prepare request data           │
│    - Set authentication             │
│    - Create preconditions           │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 2. ACT - Make the API call          │
│    - Send HTTP request              │
│    - Capture response               │
├─────────────────────────────────────┤
│ 3. ASSERT - Verify results          │
│    - Check status code              │
│    - Validate response body         │
│    - Verify headers                 │
│    - Check side effects             │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

**Example: Create User Test**:

```javascript
// ARRANGE
const userData = {
    name: "John Doe",
    email: "john" + Date.now() + "@example.com", // Unique email
    password: "Test123!"
};

// ACT
const response = await request(app)
    .post('/api/users')
    .send(userData)
    .set('Content-Type', 'application/json');

// ASSERT
assert.equal(response.status, 201);
assert.ok(response.body.id);
assert.equal(response.body.name, userData.name);
assert.equal(response.body.email, userData.email);
assert.ok(response.body.createdAt);

// Verify user was saved in database
const savedUser = await db.findUser(response.body.id);
assert.ok(savedUser);
assert.equal(savedUser.email, userData.email);
```

**What to Test in Each Response Part**:

| Component | What to Check | Examples |
|-----------|---------------|----------|
| **Status Code** | Correct HTTP status | 200, 201, 400, 401, 404 |
| **Headers** | Content-Type, auth headers, rate limits | `Content-Type: application/json` |
| **Body** | Structure, data types, values | Has ID, name matches request |
| **Timing** | Response time | < 500ms |

**Common Assertions by HTTP Method**:

| Method | Success | Common Assertions |
|--------|---------|-------------------|
| **GET** | 200 OK | Body contains data, correct structure |
| **GET (single)** | 200 OK | Has all fields, matches ID |
| **POST** | 201 Created | Has ID, Location header |
| **PUT** | 200 OK | Updated fields match |
| **PATCH** | 200 OK | Only specified fields changed |
| **DELETE** | 204 No Content | Empty body, resource gone on subsequent GET |

**Testing Error Cases**:

```javascript
// Test validation errors
describe('POST /api/users - validation', () => {
    it('returns 400 when email is invalid', async () => {
        const response = await request(app)
            .post('/api/users')
            .send({
                name: "John",
                email: "not-an-email"
            });

        expect(response.status).toBe(400);
        expect(response.body.error).toContain('email');
    });

    it('returns 400 when required field missing', async () => {
        const response = await request(app)
            .post('/api/users')
            .send({
                email: "john@example.com"  // missing name
            });

        expect(response.status).toBe(400);
        expect(response.body.errors).toHaveProperty('name');
    });
});
```

**Testing Authentication**:

```javascript
describe('GET /api/users/:id - authentication', () => {
    it('returns 401 when no token provided', async () => {
        const response = await request(app)
            .get('/api/users/123');

        expect(response.status).toBe(401);
    });

    it('returns 403 when accessing another user', async () => {
        const token = await loginAs("user1@example.com");

        const response = await request(app)
            .get('/api/users/456')  // user2's ID
            .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);

        expect(response.status).toBe(403);
    });

    it('returns 200 with valid token', async () => {
        const token = await loginAs("user1@example.com");
        const userId = getCurrentUserId();

        const response = await request(app)
            .get(`/api/users/${userId}`)
            .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);

        expect(response.status).toBe(200);
    });
});
```

**Testing Response Structure**:

```javascript
// Using JSON Schema validation
const userSchema = {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['id', 'name', 'email', 'createdAt'],
    properties: {
        id: { type: 'integer' },
        name: { type: 'string', minLength: 1 },
        email: { type: 'string', format: 'email' },
        createdAt: { type: 'string', format: 'date-time' },
        updatedAt: { type: 'string', format: 'date-time' }
    }
};

it('returns user with correct structure', async () => {
    const response = await request(app)
        .get('/api/users/123');

    expect(response.status).toBe(200);
    expect(response.body).toMatchSchema(userSchema);
});
```

**Key Insight**: Each test should verify one specific behavior. Test both success and failure cases. Use descriptive test names that explain what you're testing and what should happen.

Managing test data is one of the biggest challenges in API testing. Good test data management makes tests reliable and maintainable.

**Test Data Challenges**:

❌ Tests that pass but shouldn't (false positives)
❌ Tests that fail randomly (flaky tests)
❌ Tests that interfere with each other
❌ Slow test setup and teardown
❌ Managing test data across environments

**Test Data Strategies**:

**1. Static Test Data**

Pre-defined, fixed test data.

```javascript
// users-fixtures.js
module.exports = {
    adminUser: {
        id: 1,
        name: "Admin User",
        email: "admin@example.com",
        role: "admin"
    },
    regularUser: {
        id: 2,
        name: "John Doe",
        email: "john@example.com",
        role: "user"
    }
};
```

**Pros**: Simple, repeatable
**Cons**: Not isolated, can cause conflicts

**2. Dynamic Test Data**

Generate unique data for each test.

```javascript
function generateUser(overrides = {}) {
    const timestamp = Date.now();
    return {
        name: `Test User ${timestamp}`,
        email: `test-${timestamp}@example.com`,
        password: "Test123!",
        ...overrides
    };
}

// Usage
it('creates a user', async () => {
    const userData = generateUser({ role: 'admin' });
    // Each test gets unique data
});
```

**Pros**: Isolated, no conflicts
**Cons**: Can't predict exact values

**3. Database Seeds**

Pre-populate database with known data.

```javascript
// seeds/development/users.js
exports.seed = async function(knex) {
    await knex('users').del();
    await knex('users').insert([
        { id: 1, name: 'Admin', email: 'admin@test.com' },
        { id: 2, name: 'User 1', email: 'user1@test.com' },
        { id: 3, name: 'User 2', email: 'user2@test.com' }
    ]);
};
```

**Pros**: Known state, fast
**Cons**: Not isolated, IDs fixed

**4. Factory Pattern**

Create data factories with sensible defaults.

```javascript
// factories/userFactory.js
class UserFactory {
    constructor() {
        this.sequence = 0;
    }

    create(attrs = {}) {
        this.sequence++;
        return {
            id: this.sequence,
            name: `User ${this.sequence}`,
            email: `user${this.sequence}@example.com`,
            role: 'user',
            createdAt: new Date(),
            ...attrs
        };
    }

    createAdmin(attrs = {}) {
        return this.create({ role: 'admin', ...attrs });
    }
}
```

**5. Test Transactions**

Run each test in a transaction and roll back.

```javascript
// Jest with database transactions
beforeEach(async () => {
    await db.beginTransaction();
});

afterEach(async () => {
    await db.rollbackTransaction();
});

it('creates a user', async () => {
    // This data will be rolled back after test
    await request(app).post('/api/users').send(userData);
    // Assertions...
});
```

**Test Data Checklist**:

✅ Each test uses unique data when needed
✅ Tests clean up after themselves
✅ No test depends on another test's data
✅ Data represents real-world scenarios
✅ Edge cases are covered (empty, null, very long strings)
✅ Sensitive data is not used

**Example: Complete Test with Data Management**:

```javascript
describe('User API', () => {
    let testUser;
    let authToken;

    beforeEach(async () => {
        // Create fresh user for each test
        testUser = await createTestUser();
        authToken = await login(testUser.email, testUser.password);
    });

    afterEach(async () => {
        // Clean up
        await deleteTestUser(testUser.id);
    });

    it('gets user profile', async () => {
        const response = await request(app)
            .get(`/api/users/${testUser.id}`)
            .set('Authorization', `Bearer ${authToken}`);

        expect(response.status).toBe(200);
        expect(response.body.email).toBe(testUser.email);
    });
});

// Helper functions
async function createTestUser() {
    const userData = generateUser();
    const response = await request(app)
        .post('/api/users')
        .send(userData);
    return response.body;
}

async function deleteTestUser(userId) {
    await db('users').where({ id: userId }).del();
}
```

**Key Insight**: Good test data management is invisible. Tests should be independent, repeatable, and not interfere with each other. Invest time in good test data factories and cleanup routines.

