**Boundary Testing Best Practices**:

✅ Test both sides of every boundary
✅ Test exact boundary values (min, max)
✅ Test values just outside boundaries
✅ Test zero and negative where applicable
✅ Test empty and null for strings/arrays
✅ Test maximum lengths for all string fields
✅ Test minimum and maximum for all numeric fields

**Boundary Value Analysis Table**:

| Field Type | Test Values | Expected |
|------------|-------------|----------|
| Integer 1-100 | 0, 1, 2, 99, 100, 101 | 0,101 invalid; rest valid |
| String 2-50 | len=1,2,25,50,51 | 1,51 invalid; rest valid |
| Array 1-10 | size=0,1,5,10,11 | 0,11 invalid; rest valid |
| Decimal 0.01-1000 | 0, 0.01, 999.99, 1000, 1000.01 | 0,1000.01 invalid; rest valid |
| Date future | past, today, today+1, 5yrs+1 | past, 5yrs+1 invalid |

**Key Insight**: Most bugs happen at boundaries. If your API works at the edges, it will likely work in the middle.

APIs must handle malformed requests gracefully. Invalid JSON, XML, or other formats should never crash your server or expose sensitive information.

**Types of Malformed Payloads**:

| Type | Example | Risk |
|------|---------|------|
| **Invalid JSON** | `{name: "John"}` (missing quotes) | Parsing errors |
| **Truncated JSON** | `{"name": "John` (incomplete) | Parser hangs |
| **JSON with comments** | `{"name": /*comment*/ "John"}` | Unexpected tokens |
| **JSON with trailing commas** | `{"name": "John",}` | Strict parsers fail |
| **Very deep nesting** | `{"a":{"b":{"c":...}}}` | Stack overflow |
| **Extremely large payload** | 100MB JSON | DoS risk |
| **Invalid XML** | `<root><name>John</root` (unclosed) | Parser errors |
| **XML with entities** | `<!ENTITY xxe SYSTEM "file:///etc/passwd">` | XXE attacks |

**Testing Invalid JSON**:

